```kotlin
suspend fun likePost(postId: String) {
    val previous = localDb.getPost(postId)
    localDb.updatePost(postId) { it.copy(liked = true, likeCount = it.likeCount + 1) }
    // UI already re-rendered with the optimistic state by this point

    try {
        api.likePost(postId)
    } catch (e: ApiException) {
        localDb.updatePost(postId) { previous }   // roll back to the pre-optimistic state
        showError("Couldn't like post — try again")
    }
}
```

The pattern has three parts, and skipping any one of them is the common bug: snapshot the pre-change state, apply the optimistic change immediately, and roll back to the snapshot (not just "undo the specific field") if the server rejects it — a rollback that doesn't restore the exact previous state can leave the local data in a state that never actually existed on the server or the client.
